A||||6||EdsB52||Not just another North* ?||||1||
Z||000000||EdsB52||07-24-2001||11:30 PM||||The 1999 Caddy DeVille with a Northstar is rated at 275HP while the SeVille is rated at 300, what's the difference? I'm thinking exhaust or cams or?, please help. Thanks, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||07-24-2001||11:38 PM||||The main difference is the intake cam.<BR>There might be a compression ratio difference too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||kslish||07-24-2001||11:47 PM||||I believe the computer chips in the ECM are different.  There may also be some other tuning differences that I am not familiar with (cam maybe?).  I do know that the DeVille engine is tuned for more low end torque while the Seville engine is tuned for overall horsepower.<P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||07-25-2001||04:21 AM||||Thanks for the replies. Do you think the differences are major? Is the 25 HP loss noticeable? Thx.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hym-The-Man||07-25-2001||05:27 AM||||Pontiaddict is right the main diff is the intake cam.  I do believe that you would feel a difference in overall acceleration, but not that much.  You should really be concerned about torque if it is off the line acceleration you are looking for.  A N* in the 300 hp range is 295 lb. ft. of torque, but the 275 hp N* is rated at 300 lb. ft.  Check it out at:  <A HREF="http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/northstar/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/northstar/index.htm</A> <P>So as far as off the line you would actually be quicker with the 275.<BR>My suggestion is to have the heads ported and polished an call it a day.  That would make more than 300 hp and waaaaay more than 30 lb. ft. of torque.<P>When I get the money I am going with a Aurora engine.  There is a salvage yard around here that regularly has them in stock.  They compression test them and are all in good shape.  I hope that helps<P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hym-The-Man||07-25-2001||05:34 AM||||Pontiaddict is right the main diff is the intake cam.  I do believe that you would feel a difference in overall acceleration, but not that much.  You should really be concerned about torque if it is off the line acceleration you are looking for.  A N* in the 300 hp range is 295 lb. ft. of torque, but the 275 hp N* is rated at 300 lb. ft.  Check it out at:  <A HREF="http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/northstar/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gm.com/automotive/gmpowertrain/engines_cartruck/northstar/index.htm</A> <P>So as far as off the line you would actually be quicker with the 275.<BR>My suggestion is to have the heads ported and polished an call it a day.  That would make more than 300 hp and waaaaay more than 30 lb. ft. of torque.<P>When I get the money I am going with a Aurora engine.  There is a salvage yard around here that regularly has them in stock.  They compression test them and are all in good shape.  I hope that helps<P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||07-25-2001||10:24 AM||||You guys ar basically right. I checked with Kecin at Design1 and he wrote;<P>Ed, the differences are the rear end ratio and the torque at the bottom end<BR>of the RPM band.  The 300HP with a 3.71 final drive pulls out of the hole<BR>better but actually has less torque below 5000 RPM that the 275HP which has<BR>a 3.11 final drive.  At highway speeds the 275 runs at 20% less rpm and<BR>still pulls extremely well.  Since the Fiero is about 1200 lbs. lighter that<BR>the Cadillac the 275HP engine is a good choice and actually my choice for<BR>everyday driving and taking trips.  If you plan to do street racing or drag<BR>racing then the 300HP is probably a better choice.  When our performance<BR>upgrades are available in the near future the differences will be<BR>negligible.  The mechanical difference between the engine is in the intake<BR>camshaft and valve spring preset.  Therefore the calibrations in the prom<BR>are different.  The PCM is identical.  Some of out customers want the 300HP<BR>for "bragging rights" but are usually more costly than the 275HP.  We are<BR>experimenting with a 300HP using a 3.11 final drive on a car we just<BR>finished.  What year and body style(Deville, Eldorado, Seville) is the Caddy<BR>you found?<BR>Kevin Leslie<BR>Design 1 Systems<BR>3411 Noma Road<BR>Oklahoma City, OK 73150-2003<BR>405-733-5505 voice<BR>405-733-7725 fax<BR>kevin@design1systems.com <A HREF="http://www.design1systems.com" TARGET=_blank>www.design1systems.com</A> <P>Thanks for all replies. I found a 275HP fairly cheap, with the whole car. It runs beautifully, an fact, you can barly hear the engine run or accelerate even with the hood open. You could set a glass of water on it and probably not even get a jiggle accelerating to 5,000rpm. The thing I like is that it is in the car reading 18,000 miles, not on the junkyard shelf with the proverbial "20,000 mile" jack-me-off from the junkyard salesman. If I don't buy it, I'll pass it on to you guys.||||reg||1
